WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:09.140
Were Trump supporters a monolithic
group of people with identical opinions?

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:13.679
The Democracy Fund Voter Study Group recently
looked at the data, and Dr. Emily Ekins

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:18.119
identified five separate clusters of his
supporters. Staunch conservatives were

00:00:18.119 --> 00:00:22.529
the largest group. These people are loyal
Republicans, solid fiscal conservatives,

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:27.000
and have traditional values. A second
group of his supporters – free marketeers –

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:31.650
favor smaller government, have moderate
to liberal social views, and support free

00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:35.790
trade. The third group are what Ekins
called the "anti-elite". These people are

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:39.450
more progressive and see the economic
and political system stacked against

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:44.399
them. Still another group, the disengaged,
are only about 5% of his supporters. A

00:00:44.399 --> 00:00:48.300
final group, what Ekins called the
American preservationist might be the

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:52.050
most significant for Trump because they
propelled him to the nomination. They

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:56.190
support higher taxes on the rich, like
the social safety net, and are free trade

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:58.980
skeptics. There is overlap among the groups, but

00:00:58.980 --> 00:01:04.470
the big takeaway from Ekins' analysis is
that there is no such thing as a single

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.360
kind of Trump voter.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.900
